1
00:00:00,338 --> 00:00:02,381
Look down at what you're
wearing right now.

2
00:00:02,401 --> 00:00:03,931
A shirt. Some trousers.

3
00:00:03,951 --> 00:00:08,849
Maybe socks. Every day, almost
every person on Earth gets dressed.

4
00:00:08,869 --> 00:00:13,899
The world now buys more than a hundred
billion new pieces of clothing every year.

5
00:00:13,919 --> 00:00:17,187
About twice as many as in
the year two thousand.

6
00:00:17,207 --> 00:00:21,837
In the US, that works out to roughly
sixty nine new pieces of clothing per

7
00:00:21,857 --> 00:00:23,742
person, every year.

8
00:00:23,762 --> 00:00:25,604
But here's something strange.

9
00:00:25,624 --> 00:00:27,629
You're the only animal that does this.

10
00:00:27,649 --> 00:00:29,304
Dogs don't get dressed.

11
00:00:29,324 --> 00:00:31,492
Cats don't. Chimps don't.

12
00:00:31,512 --> 00:00:34,581
No other animal on the
planet makes clothes.

13
00:00:34,601 --> 00:00:35,680
So when did we start?

14
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:39,538
And why? Now your brain
is probably saying, easy.

15
00:00:39,558 --> 00:00:42,726
It got cold, so somebody
grabbed an animal skin.

16
00:00:42,746 --> 00:00:45,510
Done. At least, that's what we assume.

17
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:46,548
Here's the problem.

18
00:00:46,568 --> 00:00:50,273
Clothes rot. Leather, fur, grass, string.

19
00:00:50,293 --> 00:00:52,898
Give them a few thousand
years, and they're gone.

20
00:00:52,918 --> 00:00:56,939
So how could anyone know when
the very first outfit was worn?

21
00:00:56,959 --> 00:01:00,426
The answer turned up in the
strangest place imaginable.

22
00:01:00,446 --> 00:01:02,939
To get there, we'll follow three clues.

23
00:01:02,959 --> 00:01:04,939
Your skin. A tiny bug.

24
00:01:04,959 --> 00:01:06,249
And a needle.

25
00:01:06,269 --> 00:01:08,236
Clue number one is your own skin.

26
00:01:08,256 --> 00:01:09,998
Look at a chimpanzee's arm.

27
00:01:10,018 --> 00:01:11,573
Thick, dark hair, everywhere.

28
00:01:11,593 --> 00:01:12,932
Now look at yours.

29
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:14,307
But here's the twist.

30
00:01:14,327 --> 00:01:19,494
Up close, a patch of your skin has about
as many hair follicles as a chimp's.

31
00:01:19,514 --> 00:01:24,210
Most of yours just grow tiny,
fine hairs that are hard to see.

32
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:28,622
So at some point, our ancestors' fur
got thinner, and thinner, until it was

33
00:01:28,642 --> 00:01:34,210
nearly gone. Some scientists think Homo
erectus, more than one and a half million

34
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:38,270
years ago, was among the
first with bare, sweaty skin.

35
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:40,888
Why would anyone give up a free fur coat?

36
00:01:40,908 --> 00:01:42,913
The leading idea is heat.

37
00:01:42,933 --> 00:01:48,001
Our ancestors lived in hot, open country,
walking and running after food for

38
00:01:48,021 --> 00:01:50,474
hours. And fur traps heat.

39
00:01:50,494 --> 00:01:52,580
So instead of fur, we got sweat.

40
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:54,393
We are the sweatiest ape.

41
00:01:54,413 --> 00:01:59,230
A patch of your skin has about ten times
more sweat glands than the same patch on

42
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:03,480
a chimp. On a hot day, you can
sweat more than a litre an hour.

43
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:08,118
Sweat on bare skin, plus a breeze,
works like built-in air conditioning.

44
00:02:08,138 --> 00:02:09,930
That's the leading idea, at least.

45
00:02:09,950 --> 00:02:13,673
It isn't proven. But bare
skin has a new problem.

46
00:02:13,693 --> 00:02:17,913
The sun. So the skin underneath
got darker, to protect itself.

47
00:02:17,933 --> 00:02:21,525
Geneticists found that
change written in our DNA.

48
00:02:21,545 --> 00:02:24,713
A gene for dark, sun-protecting skin.

49
00:02:24,733 --> 00:02:30,013
It suggests our ancestors had mostly bare
skin by at least one point two million

50
00:02:30,033 --> 00:02:32,000
years ago. Maybe earlier.

51
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:34,590
So by then, most of their fur was gone.

52
00:02:34,610 --> 00:02:37,235
And for a very long time,
that's how they lived.

53
00:02:37,255 --> 00:02:38,848
No shirts. No shoes.

54
00:02:38,868 --> 00:02:40,191
No problem.

55
00:02:40,211 --> 00:02:42,116
Which brings us to clue number two.

56
00:02:42,136 --> 00:02:44,660
It's tiny. And a little bit gross.

57
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:49,689
It's a louse. Humans can get head lice,
which live in the hair on your head.

58
00:02:49,709 --> 00:02:53,901
And clothing lice, which can't
survive on bare skin for long.

59
00:02:53,921 --> 00:02:55,489
They live in clothes.

60
00:02:55,509 --> 00:03:00,101
They cling to the fabric, lay their eggs
along the seams, and only crawl onto the

61
00:03:00,121 --> 00:03:03,035
skin to feed. Think about what that means.

62
00:03:03,055 --> 00:03:07,022
Clothing lice could only exist once
there were clothes to live in.

63
00:03:07,042 --> 00:03:09,692
No clothes, no clothing lice.

64
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:14,042
So if you could find out when clothing
lice first appeared, you'd know roughly

65
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:15,924
when clothes appeared.

66
00:03:15,944 --> 00:03:21,099
In two thousand three, a team led by the
geneticist Mark Stoneking set out to do

67
00:03:21,119 --> 00:03:26,464
exactly that. They compared the
DNA of head lice and clothing lice.

68
00:03:26,484 --> 00:03:31,389
DNA picks up small changes over
time, at a fairly steady rate.

69
00:03:31,409 --> 00:03:32,952
Like a ticking clock.

70
00:03:32,972 --> 00:03:37,951
Count the differences, and you can work
out how long ago two groups split apart.

71
00:03:37,971 --> 00:03:42,676
A bigger study in twenty eleven found that
clothing lice split from head lice at

72
00:03:42,696 --> 00:03:45,451
least eighty three thousand years ago.

73
00:03:45,471 --> 00:03:49,676
And possibly as early as a
hundred and seventy thousand.

74
00:03:49,696 --> 00:03:53,186
So people were probably wearing
clothes regularly by then.

75
00:03:53,206 --> 00:03:55,449
Think about how wild that is.

76
00:03:55,469 --> 00:04:00,387
One of the greatest inventions in
human history was dated using lice.

77
00:04:00,407 --> 00:04:04,315
So next time someone says lice
are useless, you'll know better.

78
00:04:04,335 --> 00:04:05,547
And look at the gap.

79
00:04:05,567 --> 00:04:09,260
Mostly bare skin by one
point two million years ago.

80
00:04:09,280 --> 00:04:13,135
Clues to clothing, only in the
last couple of hundred thousand.

81
00:04:13,155 --> 00:04:17,835
So people in warm Africa may have managed
with little or no clothing for hundreds

82
00:04:17,855 --> 00:04:19,297
of thousands of years.

83
00:04:19,317 --> 00:04:23,471
Though fur and leather rarely
survive, so we can't be sure.

84
00:04:23,491 --> 00:04:28,033
So be honest. Could you survive one
winter night with no clothes at all?

85
00:04:28,053 --> 00:04:29,871
Tell me in the comments.

86
00:04:29,891 --> 00:04:32,259
So why did people finally get dressed?

87
00:04:32,279 --> 00:04:34,520
One big idea is the cold.

88
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:40,433
Our planet has swung between warm spells
and long, cold glacial periods, again

89
00:04:40,453 --> 00:04:45,674
and again. In the cold times, temperatures
dropped and winters got brutal.

90
00:04:45,694 --> 00:04:50,924
The archaeologist Ian Gilligan argues
that clothing began mainly as protection

91
00:04:50,944 --> 00:04:52,824
from Ice Age cold.

92
00:04:52,844 --> 00:04:57,049
He even suggests that the need for
cloth may have helped push people toward

93
00:04:57,069 --> 00:05:00,232
farming. Though that idea is debated.

94
00:05:00,252 --> 00:05:04,007
But turning an animal into a
coat is harder than it sounds.

95
00:05:04,027 --> 00:05:08,557
You have to skin it, scrape it clean,
stretch it out to dry, and work it until

96
00:05:08,577 --> 00:05:11,921
it's soft. Or it rots, and it stinks.

97
00:05:11,941 --> 00:05:14,188
And that's where clue
number three comes in.

98
00:05:14,208 --> 00:05:19,606
In a cave in Morocco, archaeologists
found sixty two bone tools.

99
00:05:19,626 --> 00:05:22,656
Shaped and polished for
working leather and fur.

100
00:05:22,676 --> 00:05:27,196
They're about ninety thousand to a
hundred and twenty thousand years old.

101
00:05:27,216 --> 00:05:31,756
In the same cave were the bones
of foxes, jackals, and wildcats.

102
00:05:31,776 --> 00:05:34,206
With careful cut marks from skinning.

103
00:05:34,226 --> 00:05:36,280
There's not much meat on a fox.

104
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:38,593
They probably wanted the fur.

105
00:05:38,613 --> 00:05:41,443
Even Neanderthals twisted string.

106
00:05:41,463 --> 00:05:46,930
A tiny scrap of three-ply cord, stuck to
a stone tool in France, is about forty

107
00:05:46,950 --> 00:05:49,698
one to fifty two thousand years old.

108
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:52,786
Then came a tiny invention
that changed everything.

109
00:05:52,806 --> 00:05:57,029
The needle. With a needle and
thread, you can sew pieces together.

110
00:05:57,049 --> 00:05:59,154
A body. Sleeves. A hood.

111
00:05:59,174 --> 00:06:01,835
Trousers. Clothes that actually fit.

112
00:06:01,855 --> 00:06:06,898
The oldest needles with an eye are about
forty thousand years old, from a cave in

113
00:06:06,918 --> 00:06:12,428
Siberia. Before that, people probably
punched holes with pointed bone awls.

114
00:06:12,448 --> 00:06:17,436
In the Czech Republic, people even pressed
woven material into clay, about twenty

115
00:06:17,456 --> 00:06:21,122
six thousand years ago,
leaving its pattern behind.

116
00:06:21,142 --> 00:06:26,059
And sewn clothes took people to
places no human had lived before.

117
00:06:26,079 --> 00:06:30,459
By about thirty two thousand years ago,
people were living far north of the

118
00:06:30,479 --> 00:06:32,797
Arctic Circle, in Siberia.

119
00:06:32,817 --> 00:06:36,753
And they left behind nearly
two hundred bone needles.

120
00:06:36,773 --> 00:06:40,478
Neanderthals, our close cousins,
worked animal hides too.

121
00:06:40,498 --> 00:06:44,553
They probably wore draped capes
rather than sewn, fitted clothes.

122
00:06:44,573 --> 00:06:46,466
But that's educated guesswork.

123
00:06:46,486 --> 00:06:50,628
One model suggests some Neanderthal groups
may have needed to cover up to eighty

124
00:06:50,648 --> 00:06:52,653
percent of their bodies in winter.

125
00:06:52,673 --> 00:06:57,756
Some researchers think fitted clothes gave
our species an edge in the deep cold.

126
00:06:57,776 --> 00:07:00,631
And here's a clue hiding in your own feet.

127
00:07:00,651 --> 00:07:02,556
Shoes rarely survive.

128
00:07:02,576 --> 00:07:07,856
But toe bones do. Wearing shoes for years
leaves your little toe bones weaker and

129
00:07:07,876 --> 00:07:12,006
thinner. And in people from about forty
thousand years ago, they're already

130
00:07:12,026 --> 00:07:15,378
thinner. Your toes still
remember the first shoes.

131
00:07:15,398 --> 00:07:17,578
But here's the part most people miss.

132
00:07:17,598 --> 00:07:20,774
Clothes were never only
about staying warm.

133
00:07:20,794 --> 00:07:26,063
In a cave in Georgia called Dzudzuana,
scientists found flax fibres from more

134
00:07:26,083 --> 00:07:29,863
than thirty thousand years
ago, some twisted into thread.

135
00:07:29,883 --> 00:07:31,951
And some seem to have been coloured.

136
00:07:31,971 --> 00:07:34,063
Black. Grey. Turquoise.

137
00:07:34,083 --> 00:07:36,693
And in a later layer, one pink.

138
00:07:36,713 --> 00:07:40,334
Tens of thousands of years
ago, someone wanted pink.

139
00:07:40,354 --> 00:07:45,894
And the oldest known blue dye on cloth is
indigo, on cotton from Peru, about six

140
00:07:45,914 --> 00:07:47,442
thousand years old.

141
00:07:47,462 --> 00:07:52,067
Little carved figures, around twenty five
to thirty thousand years old, seem to

142
00:07:52,087 --> 00:07:55,292
show woven caps, and
skirts made of string.

143
00:07:55,312 --> 00:07:59,279
Some think the famous Venus of
Willendorf wears a woven cap.

144
00:07:59,299 --> 00:08:01,380
Others see braided hair.

145
00:08:01,400 --> 00:08:06,855
At Sungir, in Russia, about thirty four
thousand years ago, a man and two boys

146
00:08:06,875 --> 00:08:12,192
were buried in clothes covered with more
than thirteen thousand ivory beads.

147
00:08:12,212 --> 00:08:15,767
By one estimate, each bead
took about an hour to make.

148
00:08:15,787 --> 00:08:18,691
That's nearly ten thousand hours of work.

149
00:08:18,711 --> 00:08:23,153
And decoration may be even older
than our oldest clues to clothing.

150
00:08:23,173 --> 00:08:28,328
Shell beads from Morocco are at least a
hundred and forty two thousand years old.

151
00:08:28,348 --> 00:08:31,853
So from very early on, what you
wore said something about you.

152
00:08:31,873 --> 00:08:34,629
Who you were. Where you belonged.

153
00:08:34,649 --> 00:08:38,379
Here's what a full outfit looked like,
about five thousand three hundred years

154
00:08:38,399 --> 00:08:42,066
ago. Meet Ötzi, a man
found frozen in the Alps.

155
00:08:42,086 --> 00:08:45,516
A bearskin hat. A patchwork
coat of sheep and goat hide.

156
00:08:45,536 --> 00:08:46,741
Goatskin leggings.

157
00:08:46,761 --> 00:08:48,279
Laces of cattle leather.

158
00:08:48,299 --> 00:08:52,304
And deerskin shoes, stuffed with
dry grass to keep his feet warm.

159
00:08:52,324 --> 00:08:55,538
At least five kinds of
animal, in one outfit.

160
00:08:55,558 --> 00:08:59,888
One shoemaker made copies of Ötzi
's shoes and went hiking in them.

161
00:08:59,908 --> 00:09:02,976
He said they were better
than most modern footwear.

162
00:09:02,996 --> 00:09:05,552
Good grip. No blisters.

163
00:09:05,572 --> 00:09:10,506
The oldest known woven garment is a
linen shirt from Egypt, with a V-neck and

164
00:09:10,526 --> 00:09:14,043
pleated sleeves, about
five thousand years old.

165
00:09:14,063 --> 00:09:18,493
It was dug up in nineteen thirteen, but
nobody noticed it until nineteen seventy

166
00:09:18,513 --> 00:09:21,614
seven, in a bag of old, dirty linen.

167
00:09:21,634 --> 00:09:26,639
And trousers? Some of the oldest known
pairs are about three thousand years old,

168
00:09:26,659 --> 00:09:28,201
from western China.

169
00:09:28,221 --> 00:09:30,327
They were worn by horse riders.

170
00:09:30,347 --> 00:09:33,844
Trousers may have been
invented for riding horses.

171
00:09:33,864 --> 00:09:38,744
And in Armenia, archaeologists found a
leather shoe about five thousand five

172
00:09:38,764 --> 00:09:42,394
hundred years old, laced
up and stuffed with grass.

173
00:09:42,414 --> 00:09:45,644
The grass was probably there
to help it keep its shape.

174
00:09:45,664 --> 00:09:48,294
It's about a modern size thirty seven.

175
00:09:48,314 --> 00:09:50,695
It would almost fit someone today.

176
00:09:50,715 --> 00:09:56,557
Even older are sandals woven from
sagebrush bark, found in a cave in Oregon.

177
00:09:56,577 --> 00:10:00,212
About nine to ten and a
half thousand years old.

178
00:10:00,232 --> 00:10:02,725
So why did humans start wearing clothes?

179
00:10:02,745 --> 00:10:06,225
First, we lost our fur,
probably to stay cool.

180
00:10:06,245 --> 00:10:08,862
Then the cold made
clothes worth the effort.

181
00:10:08,882 --> 00:10:11,175
And then clothes became something more.

182
00:10:11,195 --> 00:10:15,333
Who we are. Before we go,
which clue surprised you most?

183
00:10:15,353 --> 00:10:18,158
Your hair follicles,
the lice, or your toes?

184
00:10:18,178 --> 00:10:20,072
Let me know in the comments.

185
00:10:20,092 --> 00:10:23,885
Every video on this channel
asks one kind of question.

186
00:10:23,905 --> 00:10:27,869
How did humans live without the
things we can't live without?

187
00:10:27,889 --> 00:10:32,857
Every time you pull on a sock, you're
using an invention older than farming,

188
00:10:32,877 --> 00:10:35,873
older than writing, older than cities.

189
00:10:35,893 --> 00:10:39,323
Tomorrow morning, you'll get
dressed in about two minutes.

190
00:10:39,343 --> 00:10:43,818
It took humanity hundreds of
thousands of years to learn how.
